function sections(t: Topic) {
  return [
    { heading: 'A route-local operating guide', body: t.sourceBody },
    { heading: `Why ${t.lens} needs a narrow lane`, body: `A useful offshore legal support role begins with a defined administrative result. For ${t.lens}, the firm should name the matter types, source records, expected handoff, and person who reviews the work. The worker is not being asked to replace counsel. The worker is being asked to make a repeatable part of the file easier to see and easier to check. That distinction matters because ${t.risk}. A broad instruction such as “keep this organized” leaves the worker to invent priorities. A bounded instruction says what enters the queue, what leaves it, and what must stop. The firm can then test the process on a small sample before giving it more volume or wider access.` },
    { heading: 'Define the source boundary', body: `Write the input rule before opening a system. In this workflow, the approved inputs are ${t.input}. Record where each source came from, when it was received, and whether it is complete enough for preparation. Do not repair an unclear source by guessing. If a name, date, identity, or instruction conflicts with another record, preserve both records and describe the conflict in plain language. The first deliverable is not a polished conclusion; it is a trustworthy trail from source to prepared item. Use named accounts, matter-level permissions, and a current example. A worker who can see only the assigned material is less likely to solve an unclear task by searching everywhere.` },
    { heading: 'Design the handoff', body: `The finished administrative output for this lane is ${t.output}. Give it a stable name and a predictable location. Include the source links, the date checked, the preparer, and a short list of anything that could not be resolved. The reviewer should not have to reconstruct the worker’s path from chat messages or memory. The handoff should answer four questions: what was requested, what was found, what remains uncertain, and who owns the decision. If the result is going to a client, court, vendor, or opposing party, place a separate release gate before it leaves the firm. Preparation can be offshore; the firm’s authorized reviewer controls the meaning and release.` },
    { heading: 'Separate facts from decisions', body: `The safest writing style is factual and traceable. “The notice lists May 4” is different from “May 4 is the legal deadline.” “The file contains two versions” is different from “the second version controls.” “The client asked about timing” is different from “the firm promised a response.” Use labels such as source found, missing, conflicting, awaiting review, and instruction received. Avoid labels that imply legal sufficiency, privilege, compliance, urgency, or liability unless the firm supplied the exact authorized decision. This makes the worker’s contribution useful without expanding the role into legal judgment. It also gives the reviewer a clear place to correct the record.` },
    { heading: 'Build an exception route', body: `An exception is a work item, not an inconvenience to hide. For ${t.lens}, common exceptions include an incomplete source, an unfamiliar party name, a duplicate record, a changed instruction, an access problem, or a request that falls outside the approved example. The worker should pause, preserve the relevant evidence, state the question, and send it to ${t.owner}. The escalation record should show when it was sent and whether anyone acknowledged it. Do not create a second unofficial queue in a personal notebook. Do not forward confidential material to an unapproved recipient in order to get an answer faster. A visible exception queue lets the firm improve instructions while protecting the matter.` },
    { heading: 'Run a measured first week', body: `Start with ${t.first}. On the first day, explain the task boundary, accounts, approved sources, naming rule, and stop rule. On the second day, observe a practice item and ask the worker to narrate which source supports each field. On the third day, allow a small live batch with same-day review. On the fourth day, compare repeated questions and update the guide rather than relying on verbal reminders. On the fifth day, review accuracy, missing details, turnaround, escalation quality, and access discipline. Add volume only when the existing lane is steady. A quick worker who silently guesses is a larger risk than a careful worker who raises a precise question.` },
    { heading: 'Keep review meaningful', body: `Review should test the things that can harm a legal matter, not merely whether the spreadsheet looks tidy. For this topic, ${t.review}. Sample both clean items and exceptions. Keep the original input beside the prepared output so the reviewer can distinguish a worker error from an ambiguous instruction. Record rework reasons in specific terms: wrong source, omitted field, unsupported inference, naming error, late escalation, or access issue. Do not turn a count of completed items into a quality claim. The firm should decide when review can move from every item to a sample, and should return to full review when the task, tool, matter type, or staff member changes.` },
    { heading: 'Protect confidentiality in ordinary work', body: `Confidentiality is part of the daily design. Use individual credentials, multifactor authentication where available, limited folders, approved transfer methods, and a clear screen and download rule. Keep training examples redacted when possible. Do not paste client facts into an unapproved tool to make a task easier. If the worker sees the wrong matter, recipient, or permission, stop and report the time, location, and action taken. Do not delete evidence or quietly correct an access problem. The firm decides whether to lock an account, preserve records, notify anyone, or follow a broader incident procedure. The offshore location does not remove the firm’s duty to supervise the work and protect client information.` },
    { heading: 'Use a practical manager scorecard', body: `A short scorecard keeps the manager close to the real workflow. Track whether the item had the right source, whether required fields were captured, whether the output matched the approved format, whether exceptions were escalated, and whether the handoff arrived within the agreed working window. Add one field for the reason for rework and one for the reviewer’s next instruction. For ${t.lens}, do not reward a worker for closing an item that should have been paused. Ask instead whether the worker recognized the boundary and made the question easy to answer. The scorecard is for coaching and control; it is not a public performance claim or a substitute for attorney review.` },
    { heading: 'Questions the firm should settle', body: `${t.question} Also settle which systems are in scope, which hours require coverage, who handles an unanswered escalation, how access is removed, and what happens when the task changes. Put those answers in the role brief and the operating guide. A candidate should be evaluated on careful source handling, written communication, consistency, and willingness to stop when an instruction is unclear. The final decision remains with the firm’s authorized people. When the role is defined this way, offshore support can provide dependable preparation and visibility while legal advice, strategy, approval, and client responsibility stay where the firm has placed them.` },
  ];
}

